  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This research systematically benchmarks machine-learning methods for predicting in-silico spatially resolved gene expression from H&E histology images, evaluating their performance, stability, translational applicability, and spatial, biological, and downstream-analysis metrics to guide users and developers.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A field with costly SRT data, routine H&E histology, multiple machine-learning approaches for SGE prediction, developer-led evaluations, differing assessment activities, and reviews lacking benchmarking needs an independent, systematic benchmark spanning technical and downstream criteria."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) Current evaluations of methods for predicting SGE from H&E are often limited, often non-independent, and mainly developer-led."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) The literature shows a benchmarking gap for SGE-from-H&E methods: existing reviews have not benchmarked the methods, while individual studies have used differing assessment activities such as external TCGA validation, ablation studies, architecture comparisons, and application demonstrations."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Because SRT remains costly while H&E histology images are cost-effective and routine, the existence of machine-learning approaches for predicting in-silico spatially resolved gene expression from H&E data provides a plausible route for extracting spatial gene-expression information from routine histology."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Several machine-learning methods have been developed to predict in-silico spatially resolved gene expression from H&E data, and CNNs and Transformers are commonly selected architectures for extracting local and global two-dimensional vision features in these methods."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Existing general reviews of SRT-related analysis fall short of the comprehensive assessment expected for an emerging field because they surveyed and categorized methods for predicting SGE from H&E without benchmarking them."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Evaluations of methods for predicting SGE from H&E are often limited in nature, often not independent, and mainly conducted by the method developers."];
  "n_cost_case" [label="(3,0,0) Currently there is: New SRT technologies hold promise for producing new insights, but remain costly, preventing their direct adoption into routine clinical use at this stage."];
  "n_cost_common" [label="(4,0,0) The common pattern is that H&E histopathology images are more cost-effective and are routinely used in clinical practice."];
  "n_cost_concl" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given Currently SRT is costly preventing clinical use, and the common is that H&E is more cost-effective and routine, therefore SRT is costly, while H&E is cost-effective and routinely used in clinical practice."];
  "n_limited_case" [label="(19,0,0) Currently there is: Evaluation studies that are presented when proposing a new method are limited in nature and often not independent."];
  "n_limited_common" [label="(20,0,0) The common pattern is that among the current literature, methods for predicting SGE from H&E have been mainly assessed and evaluated for their efficacy by their developers."];
  "n_methods_case" [label="(7,0,0) Currently there is: Several approaches have been developed to predict in silico spatially resolved gene expression (SGE) patterns using H&E data alone."];
  "n_methods_common" [label="(8,0,0) The common pattern is that among these methods, Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN) and Transformers are commonly selected architectures for extracting local and global 2D vision features around each sequenced spot from corresponding histology image patches; some methods further implemented extra components including super-resolution enhancement of SGE and Graph Neural Networks (GNN) to capture neighbourhood relationships between adjacent spots; an exemplar module had been used to guide predi..."];
  "n_reviews_case" [label="(13,0,0) Currently there is: While there exist general reviews on various aspects of SRT analysis, they have only surveyed and categorised methods predicting SGE from H&E without any benchmarking."];
  "n_reviews_common" [label="(0,0,0) The common pattern in scientific reviews for emerging fields is that they survey, categorise and include benchmarking for comprehensive assessment."];
  "n_specific_case" [label="(22,0,0) Currently there is: For instance, ST-Net, DeepSpaCE, DeepPT and TCGN evaluated the performance of their models on external TCGA data to determine the generalisability of their models."];
  "n_specific_common" [label="(23,0,0) The common pattern is that Hist2ST, EGNv1, EGNv2, TCGN and THItoGene performed an ablation study; BrST-Net conducted a comparison of 10 state-of-the-art CNN and transformer architectures; iStar demonstrated the application of its predictions by performing tissue region segmentation."];
  "n_specific_concl" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given Currently some methods evaluated on TCGA, and the common pattern is that other methods performed ablation, comparison of architectures, and application demonstration, therefore different evaluation frameworks have been used to assess the methods."];
  "n_srt_case" [label="(1,0,0) Currently there is: Spatially resolved transcriptomics (SRT) data captures the spatial organisation and heterogeneity of genes in tissues at high resolution, fundamentally transforming the way we study and understand biological processes."];
  "n_srt_common" [label="(2,0,0) The common pattern from studies is that SRT has enabled the identification of key genes and pathways involved in intricate mechanisms underlying diverse biological processes and complex cellular phenomena."];
  "n_srt_concl" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given Currently there is SRT captures spatial organisation and heterogeneity and transforms study, and the common pattern is that SRT has enabled identification of key genes and pathways, therefore SRT is fundamentally transforming the way we study and understand biological processes by enabling the identification of key genes and pathways."];
